.TH PIPE 3
.SH NAME
pipe \- two-way interprocess communication
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.BI #| id .0
.BI #| id .1
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
An attach of this device names one end of a pipe with an argument of the
form
.IR id . end .
.I Id
picks the pipe, and
.I end
is zero or one, picking which of its two ends.
The two ends are byte streams cross-connected so that what one end
writes, the other end reads.
Unlike every other device in this section,
.B #|
has no place of its own in the sixteen-slot device table (see
.IR intro (3)):
no program ever writes a
.B #|
path.
The
.I pipe
system call (see
.IR pipe (2))
is the only way to a pipe.
It makes one with both ends open, attaches to each end itself, and hands
back two ordinary descriptors.
From there each end is a file like any other: it travels to a child across
.I rfork
or
.IR spawn ,
it closes with
.IR close ,
and either end may be posted in
.B /srv
(see
.IR srv (3))
to make the far side of the pipe a service.
.PP
Reading and writing carry no framing: what crosses is a stream of bytes,
not a stream of messages, and a read may return bytes from more than one
write or only part of one.
Each direction buffers 2048 bytes.
A read on an empty ring parks until the other end writes or closes.
Once the writing end has closed and the ring has drained, a read answers
zero bytes, an end of file that does not go away.
A write on a full ring parks until the other end reads enough to make
room.
Once the reading end has closed, a write fails with
.BR EPIPE .
A write already in progress when the reading end closes returns the count
that crossed before it did.
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/pipe
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (3),
.IR srv (3),
.IR pipe (2),
.IR dup (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A write to a pipe whose reading end has closed fails with
.BR EPIPE .
A read interrupted by a note fails with
.BR EINTR .
.SH BUGS
Unlike Plan 9's pipe, this one does not preserve the boundary between one
write and the next, so a protocol that depends on a read matching a write,
rather than framing its own messages, will not work across one.
